Always send a requested full HID report, and judge a delta against the batch's latest state.

When the host's copy of a controller's state fails the CRC check carried with each delta, it
asks the client for a full report (RequestFullReport) to resync. The client answers by packing
the pad's current state as a full report. But the dedup added upstream dropped any report,
full ones included, whose bytes matched the last one flushed, and the pad state usually hasn't
changed since the last send. So the full report the host asked for was silently dropped, the
host stayed on its stale state, every later delta failed the CRC check the same way, and the
input "froze": a button held at that moment stayed pressed on the host and nothing else got
through. Recovery only happened when a request happened to land in the few milliseconds after
the pad changed but before the change was sent, which stick noise eventually made happen.

IHS_HIDReportHolderAddFull now always queues the report, with no dedup, still stashing the
state so a later delta dedups against it. Deltas keep the dedup, but now compare against the
most recent state queued this batch (pendingCurrent) when there is one, not only the last state
flushed (lastSent): otherwise a delta right after a full report in the same batch - Full(Y)
then Delta(X) - would be dropped as a no-op against the stale lastSent and leave the host at Y
while the pad is at X.

Because a full report is no longer deduped, IHS_SessionHIDSendReport now clears the holders
when input streaming is off instead of returning early: a host RequestFullReport or a
device-open full report while input is disabled would otherwise accumulate unsent and grow the
per-device buffer without bound.

